On 2008-12-11, Mike <leblanc.michael@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>> Part of the reason for this is that the game is distributed as source;
>> as you've been given the source, reading it as your way of learning
>> about the game is widely considered to be a reasonable action.
>>
>> Preserving save files is different; this is working around a
>> designed-in source of difficulty in the game. �A player who does so is
>> thus not really playing the same game at all. �Just as "explore mode"
>> ascensions aren't considered to be "the real thing" by the community,
>> so for those achieved via save file preservation.
>
> To play devil's advocate, how is this argument different than, the
> game is distributed as source; as you've been given the source,
> modifying it is considered to be a reasonable action?

Obtaining information about the game's workings via source, spoiler,
or other means does not change the game you're playing--you're still
playing the same NetHack as everybody else, you've just chosen to
learn about it via an approach other than first-hand in-game
experience.

Modifying the source is a perfectly reasonable action, however it
results in your playing a different game than everybody else is
(because you've *modified it*).  Your call whether it's more
appropriate to consider this "cheating at NetHack" or "not playing
NetHack".


